web: gunicorn app:app --bind 0.0.0.0:${PORT:-5000}
worker: python worker.py --queue ${QUEUE:-default}
# Example 70: A Procfile Declaring Process Types. (co-16)
#
# A Procfile declares EVERY process type the app needs and how to start each.
# The PaaS runs each as its own supervised process set, scaling them
# independently (Example 72). This Procfile declares TWO types: a 'web' (HTTP)
# and a 'worker' (background jobs) -- one push, two runtimes. Save as ./Procfile.

# --- Line anatomy (each line: <type>: <command>) -----------------------------
# web     => the HTTP process type. The PaaS routes inbound HTTP to THIS type.
#            gunicorn serves the WSGI app; $PORT is injected by the PaaS.
# worker  => a NON-HTTP process type. The PaaS runs it but routes no traffic to
#            it -- it pulls jobs from a queue (here, $QUEUE, also PaaS-injected).
#
# --- Why multiple process types matter (co-16) -------------------------------
# A real app is rarely JUST a web server: it also has background workers,
# schedulers, etc. The Procfile names each one so the PaaS can run + scale them
# INDEPENDENTLY (scale web to 3, worker to 1) from a single deploy.
#
# --- Verify ------------------------------------------------------------------
#   ssh dokku@<box> ps:report myapp    # => lists 'web' AND 'worker', each running
#   ssh dokku@<box> ps:scale myapp web=2 worker=1   # => Example 72 scales them apart
# [verify] both process types are declared and runnable from one push (co-16 proof).